Magnetic studies. The magnetization dynamics of Mn12Ac@NU-1000 were investigated extensively using variable-frequency and variable-temperature ac susceptibility measurements. The data were collected under a zero dc field over a frequency range of $1{-}1500\mathrm{Hz}$ which revealed significant frequency dependence of the out-of-phase ac magnetic susceptibility $(\chi^{\prime\prime})$ signal, the maxima of which shift to higher temperatures with increasing frequencies (Figure 5). This behavior is typical of the slow relaxation of the magnetization known for $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ , and is in accord with the conclusion that the unique magnetic properties of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ are preserved during the incorporation process into NU-1000. Notably, additional less-intense and frequency-dependent $\chi^{\prime\prime}$ peaks at lower temperatures (below $3\mathrm{\K}$ ) were observed which are attributed to the presence of a known fast-relaxing isomer of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ in which the Jahn-Teller elongation axis of one of the $\ensuremath{\mathbf{M}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{n}}^{3+}$ centers is tilted $90^{\circ}$ with respect to the normal slow-relaxing Mn12Ac species (Figure 5b).26 Interestingly, when compared to bulk Mn12Ac (Figure S10) this isomer is more prominent in the Mn12Ac@NU-1000 composite, which may originate from a change in the chemical environment of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ upon nano-confinement in NU-1000. Similar Jahn-Teller isomerization has been reported for Mn12Ac embedded in polymer matrices,27, 28 carbon nanotubes,29 and pressureinduced environments.30 Fitting the corresponding temperature and frequency of the observed $\chi^{\prime\prime}$ maxima to the Arrhenius law, $\tau=\tau_{0}\exp(\Delta E/k_{\mathrm{B}}T)$ , where $\tau$ is the relaxation time of magnetization, $T$ is the temperature, $\Delta E$ is the activation energy, and $k_{\mathrm{B}}$ is the Boltzmann constant, resulted in an energy barrier of $\Delta E/k_{\mathrm{B}}=69(1)\mathrm{K}$ and a pre-exponential factor $\tau_{0}=2.0(4){\cdot}10^{-8}:$ s for the relaxation processes (Figure S11). These values compare favorably to the values of $70\mathrm{K}$ and $1.2{\cdot}10^{-8}$ s for bulk $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ . Additionally, the temperature dependence of $\chi_{\mathrm{dc}}$ and the real-part of the ac magnetic susceptibility $\chi^{\prime}$ were compared to rule out the interpretation that the observed SMM behavior is from a small fraction of the sample. It was found that $\chi T$ gradually converges to $\chi_{\mathrm{dc}}T$ as frequency decreases, thus suggesting that the entire paramagnetic species in Mn12Ac@NU-1000 contributes to the slow relaxation of magnetization behavior (Figure S13).
Additional magnetic characterization via variable-temperature dc analysis $\chi T$ also revealed behavior characteristic of SMMs (Figure S12). It should be noted however, that $\chi T$ values of Mn12Ac $@$ NU-1000 with 15 emu $\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at room temperature (RT) and 17.5 emu $\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ at low temperature (LT) deviate from the literature and expected values of bulk $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ . For reference, reported $\chi T$ values of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ were 19.4 (RT) and 55.6 emu $\mathrm{K\mol^{-1}}$ (LT) while values of 31.5 (RT) and 55 emu $\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ (LT) are expected for species with spin ground states of $S=10$ .31 The deviation at RT is compatible but lower than the expected value in agreement with antiferromagnetic coupling, whereas the discrepancy at LT can be assigned to the nanoconfinement of Mn12Ac inside the cavities of NU-1000 resulting in different magnetic moment orientations of Mn12Ac. The orientation problem could be trivial at RT due to thermal motion, but at LT, the magnetic moment axis could be frozen and anisotropic. It is known that $\chi T$ values at LT are very different when parallel (\~143 emu K mol-1) or perpendicular ( ${\bf\tilde{\mu}}_{\sim10}$ emu $\mathrm{Kmol^{-1}}$ ) to the crystallographic $c$ axis of a Mn12Ac single crystal.32 The LT value of 17.5 emu $\mathrm{K\mol^{-1}}$ for Mn12Ac@NU-1000 fits well within this range reported for single crystals, and is also similar to the LT value of ${\sim}24$ emu $\mathrm{K}\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ reported for Mn12Ac@CYCU-3.18
Field-dependent magnetization measurements also revealed behavior characteristic of SMMs (Figure 5c). The magnetic hysteresis at $1.8\mathrm{~K~}$ measures a sizeable coercivity of $0.8\mathrm{~T~}$ , remanence of $2~\upmu_{\mathrm{B}}$ and saturation of $5~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ at $2\mathrm{T}$ , while a narrowing of the hysteresis loop at low fields is consistent with quantum tunneling of the magnetization. Although these characteristics of hysteresis are lower than those reported for bulk Mn12Ac,31, 33 they fit well within the range reported for similar nano-confined $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ -based composites, such as Mn12Ac@EDTrA40,34
Mn12Ac@SBA(60),35 Mn12Ac@MCM-41,36 Mn12Ac@SBA-15,37 and Mn12Ac@CYCU-3. Specifically, a lower coercivity value for Mn12Ac $@$ NU-1000 with respect to bulk Mn12Ac confirms a shorter spin relaxation time for the confined Mn12Ac inside NU-1000 which is also in agreement with the slightly lower energy barrier of Mn12Ac $@$ NU-1000 vs. bulk Mn12Ac. In summary, the above detailed magnetic characterization demonstrates incontrovertibly that the general SMM behavior of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ inside NU-1000 remains largely unaffected by the nanoscale confinement while the quantification of important magnetic parameters reveals the frozen orientation of magnetic moments at low temperature.
