Thin film synthesis and characterization. Among the various existing approaches to the nanostructuring of SMMs, grafting them onto surfaces is the method that is most prominently employed. These surface composites are expected to represent the next step toward device fabrication. Surfaces of gold, silicon and graphite have been thoroughly explored as suitable substrates in this regard.15, 16 Their short-range order, lack of stability and difficulty in processing, however, create impediments to further progress in this field. Therefore, as emphasized above, SMM $@$ MOFs composites represent excellent candidates for overcoming these challenges. Since thin film growth is a key feature in enabling the expansion of MOF applications38, 39 we herein report the first study on the design and synthesis of a thin film of SMM $@$ MOFs composite guided by our previous work on bulk composites.14, 17, 18 The availability of mesoporous MOF thin-films significantly limits progress in this field, but NU-1000 represents the first MOF of a mesoporous nature available as thin films, featuring free-standing single-crystalline sub-micrometer rods of $^{<2}$ $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ thickness (Figure S14).40 Composite thin film preparation consisted of soaking NU-1000 films in a concentrated acetonitrile solution of Mn12Ac followed by multiple washings analogous to the bulk material preparation. PXRD analysis of the pristine and composite films revealed that the Bragg positions before and after soaking are unchanged, confirming the integrity of the MOF structure in thin film form (Figure 6). Most importantly, incontrovertible evidence of the formation of Mn12Ac@NU-1000 is concluded from the change in the relative peak intensity of the (100)
reflection which is further quantified by EDX analysis indicating an SMM loading capacity of 16.6 $1\mathrm{mol}\%$ or ${\sim}12.9\ \mathrm{wt}\%\$ (Section S8, Table S3). The depth-dependent magnetic structure was probed with polarized neutron reflectivity (PNR) measurements.41 Unfortunately the results were not conclusive due to the in-plane roughness of the film. At $6\mathrm{~K~}$ in a $3\mathrm{~T~}$ field, however, the spin asymmetry (determined from the split between the spin up and spin down neutron cross sections) increases to a magnitude of less than $2\%$ near the critical angle of the reflectivity at small wavevector Q (i.e., small scattering angle). This behavior is indicative of magnetic (ferromagnetic or paramagnetic) ordering of SMMs within the MOF layers though limited statistics preclude any definitive quantitative analysis of the data. In an ideal future scenario, we will be able to close this gap by preparing crystallographically oriented thin films of NU-1000 with minimal roughness by employing the layer-by-layer method, a technique that offers excellent fine control of film layer thickness.42 These issues notwithstanding, our initial studies provide excellent proof-of-concept of the adaptability of bulk composites to thin film technologies and the results bring us one step closer to device fabrication of a functional SMM $@$ MOF composite.
