## CONCLUSIONS
Although sound and logical, previous analytical techniques characterizing the controlled nanostructuring of molecular magnets in MOFs were only of an indirect nature and the evidence was rather circumstantial. In the present study we provide the first structural evidence in real space from low-dose HRTEM imaging to elucidate the controlled incorporation of SMMs into a mesoporous MOF. The results provide incontrovertible evidence of the nanostructuring effect revealing isolated SMM molecules embedded in single-molecule fashion in the transverse direction of a crystalline, long-ranged ordered porous MOF matrix. We demonstrated that the resulting magnetic composite exhibits record molar SMM loading capacities with significantly improved thermal stabilities while the magnetic bistability of SMM guest molecules is fully preserved. In addition, we have extended the formation of bulk nanostructured magnetic arrays towards thin film fabrication by presenting the first mesoporous MOF thin film magnetic composite. This initial study provides a beachhead for investigations with crystallographically oriented thin films and bodes well for the further development of methodologies that enable the read and write processes of single molecular magnetic spins contained in specific MOF film matrices. In addition, the imaging technology presented in this work is not limited to MOF materials alone, and thus opens up new avenues for structural characterization of previously inaccessible electron beam sensitive host-guest systems.
## EXPERIMENTAL SECTION
Synthesis. All reagents and solvents were used without further purifications. Pristine samples of Mn12Ac and NU-1000 were synthesized as previously reported.24,24 The purities of bulk materials were confirmed by PXRD (Figures S2, S3). The incorporation of Mn12Ac into NU-1000 was performed by adding $0.1\mathrm{\g}$ of NU-1000 to a saturated solution of $\mathbf{Mn}_{12}\mathbf{Ac}$ in dry acetonitrile under a ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The mixture was stirred at room temperature in a closed vial for 12 hours. The resulting brown powder was filtered, washed with acetonitrile until the filtrate was colorless to remove any residual SMMs (Figure S4, S5), and dried at room temperature.
HRTEM measurements. HRTEM specimens were prepared using Leica ultra-microtome to slice $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ thin slices with Diatome diamond knife. 300-mesh lacey-carbon-coated Cu grids loaded with microtomed samples were used for imaging. Low-dose HRTEM experiments were performed on a Cs-corrected FEI cubed G2 Titan 60-300 electron microscope operated at $300\mathrm{kV}$ . Specimen searching, zone axis alignment, and pre-focusing were carried out at $13000\times$ magnification. Selected area electron diffraction (SAED) patterns were obtained using a Gatan Ultra-scan CCD camera, while HRTEM images were acquired at $44000\mathrm{~x~}$ magnification by a Gatan K2 directdetection camera in electron counting mode with the dose fractionation function.
Polarized neutron reflectivity measurements. PNR data was recorded using the PBR instrument at the NIST Center for Neutron Research (Gaithersburg, MD, USA) using neutrons with a wavelength of $4.75\mathrm{\AA}$ . The sample was mounted in a closed cycle refrigerator and field cooled to 6 K. Data was collected under static conditions in a $3\mathrm{~T~}$ magnetic field applied parallel to the sample surface with a closed cycle superconducting magnet. A Fe/Si supermirror and an Al-coil spin flipper were used to select the spin state of the incident neutron beam either parallel $(+)$ to or antiparallel $(-)$ to the applied field. A second supermirror and flipper assembly was used to analyze the spin state of the scattered beam. The beam polarization was measured to be $>93\%$ . The nonspin flip cross-sections, $(\mathbb{R}^{+})$ and $(\mathbf{R}^{-})$ , are sensitive to the nuclear scattering length density depth profile, and their difference is related to the magnetic depth profile for the projection of the magnetization parallel to the applied field. The spin asymmetry, $\mathrm{SA}=\left(R^{+}-R^{-}\right)/\left(R^{+}+R^{-}\right)$ , is extremely sensitive to the magnetic depth profile and was calculated from the raw data.
Magnetic measurements. Magnetic measurements were carried out on a Quantum Design MPMS XL SQUID magnetometer over the temperature range of 1.8-300K. AC magnetic susceptibility data were collected with an oscillating measuring field of 5 Oe in the frequency range of 1-1500 Hz. All magnetic data were scaled per mole of Mn12Ac incorporated in NU-1000 $(33.9\mathrm{\mol}\%$ ) while accounting for the diamagnetic contribution from Pascal’s constants of MOF, SMM, residual solvent (5.5 acetonitrile molecules per 0.34Mn12Ac $@$ NU-1000) and sample holder.
